  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a weather-driven dip masking stronger operational fundamentals. Port Washington dropped 26% in total revenue but that's almost entirely a capacity story: you ran 6 groomers instead of the typical staffing level, and $/groomer ($408) fell 12% below your 30-day baseline of $462. Hicksville was worse on that measure\u2014only 3 groomers at $277/groomer, nearly 32% below its $408 baseline. Both stores are down week-to-date, but the DOW-adjusted expectations show the real signal: Port is only 13.2% below calendar-normalized forecast, and Hicksville is only 11.8% below. That's not performance degradation\u2014that's Thursday being a softer day. What you should be proud of: your MTD momentum is real. Port is up 69% and Hicksville up 101% versus last month's same two days, on 77% and 214% more appointments respectively. Year-to-date you're up 44% across the board. The structural capacity expansion (Port running 6.0 groomers/day vs 2.0 last month; Hicksville at 3.0 vs 1.0) is working.\n\nOne concern: add-on revenue cratered at Port (down 48% to $145), suggesting upsell discipline broke yesterday\u2014either staffing churn, urgency, or booking density. Retail spiked at Hicksville (+925%) which feels like a single big transaction, not a trend. Watch your lapse pool closely: Port has 505 dormant/lapsed customers worth $318k annually; Hicksville has 160 worth $119k. Reactivation is lower-hanging fruit than new acquisition right now.\n\nCapacity & coverage: Today you have 7 groomers at Port and 4 at Hicksville with 15.0 bather hours at Port and zero at Hicksville; next 7 days you're fielding 55 total groomer-days (38 Port, 17 Hicksville) with 76 bather hours concentrated at Port."
